\label{main_inverse_stability_theorem}
Let $\Omega$ be a smooth bounded domain in $\mathbb{R}^{n}\,(n = 2, 3)$ and let $\mathcal{O}^{(j)} \subset \Omega$ be a relatively open neighborhood of $\partial\Omega$ such that $\partial\mathcal{O}^{(j)} \setminus \partial\Omega$ is $C^{2}$. For \eqref{two_component_GPEqn_small_data}, suppose $p_{j} \in W^{11,\infty}(Q), q_{j,k} \in H^{3}(Q), \tilde{q}_{j,k} \in H^{3}(Q)$ are real-valued and that the coefficients satisfy \eqref{coefficient_smallness_assumption}-\eqref{near_the_boundary_a_priori_assumption}. Suppose that the boundary data of \eqref{two_component_GPEqn_small_data} are real-valued and satisfy \eqref{boundary_neighborhood_0}.\\ \indent Then, for $j \in \{1,2\}$, $l \in \{1,2,3\}$, and any $T^{*} \in (0, T)$, there exists $\delta_{*,j} \in (0, 1)$ such that if \eqref{two_component_GPEqn_small_data} is well-posed for all sufficiently small $\epsilon_{l} \in \mathbb{R}$, and
\begin{equation} \label{Neumann_data_measurement_0}
\delta_{j,\Gamma_{j}} \coloneqq \left\Vert \partial^{3}_{\epsilon_{1}\epsilon_{2}\epsilon_{3}}\left(\partial_{\nu}\left(u_{j,1} - u_{j,2}\right)\right)\Big|_{\epsilon_{1} = \epsilon_{2} = \epsilon_{3} = 0}\right\Vert_{L^{2}(\Sigma_{j})} \in (0,\delta_{*,j}),
\end{equation}
we have for some constants $\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2}, \alpha_{3} > 0$ that
\begin{equation} \label{main_stability_estimate_statement}
\begin{split}
\left \Vert q_{j,1} - q_{j,2} \right \Vert^{2}_{L^{2}(\Omega\setminus\mathcal{O}^{(j)} \times (0, T^{*}))}
\leq C_{j}\left[\left(\ln \delta_{j,\Gamma_{j}}^{-\alpha_{1}} \right)^{-\alpha_{2}} + \delta_{j,\Gamma_{j}}^{\alpha_{3}}\right],\\
\left \Vert \tilde{q}_{j,1} - \tilde{q}_{j,2} \right \Vert^{2}_{L^{2}(\Omega\setminus\mathcal{O}^{(j)} \times (0, T^{*}))}
\leq \tilde{C}_{j}\left[\left(\ln \delta_{j,\Gamma_{j}}^{-\alpha_{1}} \right)^{-\alpha_{2}} + \delta_{j,\Gamma_{j}}^{\alpha_{3}}\right],
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $C_{j}, \tilde{C}_{j} > 0$ are constants that depend on $\Omega, \Gamma_{j}, \mathcal{O}^{(j)}, T, T^{*}, N_{j}, M_{j}$.

\label{linear_nonlinear_coefficient_assumptions_0}
\hfill
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Our requirement that $q_{j,k},\tilde{q}_{j,k} \in H^{3}(Q)$ is related to the fact that $m = 3$ is the smallest integer for which $H^{m}(Q)$ is a Banach algebra when $\Omega$ is a smooth bounded domain in $\mathbb{R}^{2}$ or $\mathbb{R}^{3}$\footnote{See Theorem $4.39$ in \cite{AF2003}.}. For example, we will use the Banach algebra property in the proof of the well-posedness of \eqref{two_component_GPEqn} for small boundary data. The assumption $p_{j} \in W^{11,\infty}(Q)$ will later ensure that the CGO solutions we use for the linear Schr\"{o}dinger equation have sufficient regularity.
\item[(b)] The restriction that $p_{j}$ is real-valued is used in the proof of an integral identity stated in Proposition \ref{main_integral_identity}. Moreover, we stipulate $p_{j}(x,t) = p_{j}(x)$ on $\mathcal{O}^{(j)} \times (0, T)$ in order to use a unique continuation result stated in Proposition \ref{unique_continuation_result_BC2009}. The assumption that $p_{j}, q_{j,k}, \tilde{q}_{j,k}, f^{(j)}_{l}$ are real-valued along with the fact that $\partial\mathcal{O}^{(j)}\setminus\partial\Omega$ is $C^{2}$ are used in the proof of this unique continuation result. Further details about the unique continuation result and its proof are provided in Remark \ref{BC2009_unique_continuation_modifications}-\ref{real_valued_data_assumptions}.
\item[(c)] One can uniquely determine the coefficient $p_{j}$ by applying existing results in the literature to the first-order linearization defined by \eqref{heuristic_first_order_linearization}. This is discussed in Remark \ref{linear_coefficient_recovery}. A unique determination result for the coefficients $q_{j,k}, \tilde{q}_{j,k}$ is discussed in Remark \ref{unique_determination_result_0}.
\item[(d)] The effect of the nonlinear coupling in \eqref{two_component_GPEqn_small_data} is present in the quantity $\delta_{j,\Gamma_{j}}$ defined by \eqref{Neumann_data_measurement_0}. Indeed, we will see in Section \ref{rigorous_linearization} that the function defined by \eqref{heuristic_kth_order_linearization} satisfies an equation (namely \eqref{kth_order_linearization_time-dependent}) whose source term involves derivatives of the nonlinearity
\begin{equation*}
q_{j,k}(x,t)\left|u_{j,k}\right|^{2}u_{j,k} + \tilde{q}_{j,k}(x, t)|u_{\iota(j),k}|^{2}u_{j,k}
\end{equation*}
that appears in \eqref{two_component_GPEqn_small_data}. As a result, $\delta_{j,\Gamma_{j}}$ implicitly depends on both $u_{1,k}$ and $u_{2,k}$.
\end{itemize}

\label{KS2019_Lemma_2.2_statement}
Suppose $p \in W^{1,\infty}(Q)$ and $F \in H^{1}(0, T; L^{2}(\Omega))$ satisfies $F(\cdot, 0)=  0$. Then,
\begin{equation} \label{linear_equation_0_0}
\begin{cases}
(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p(x,t))v = F(x,t) \text{ on } Q,\\
v(x,t) = 0 \text{ on } \Sigma,\\
v(x,0) = 0 \text{ on } \Omega \times \{0\}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
has a unique solution $v \in C\left([0, T]; H^{2}(\Omega) \cap H^{1}_{0}(\Omega)\right)$ satisfying the estimates
\begin{equation} \label{KS2019_Lemma_2.2_estimate}
\begin{split}
\left \Vert v \right \Vert_{L^{\infty}(0, T; L^{2}(\Omega))} \leq C \left \Vert F \right \Vert_{L^{2}(Q)},\\
\left \Vert v \right \Vert_{C([0, T]; H^{2}(\Omega))} + \left \Vert v \right \Vert_{C^{1}([0, T]; L^{2}(\Omega))} \leq C \left \Vert F \right \Vert_{H^{1}(0, T; L^{2}(\Omega))}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
for some constant $C = C\left(\Omega, T, \left\Vert p \right\Vert_{W^{1,\infty}(Q)}\right) > 0$.

\label{linear_well-posedness}
Let $\Omega$ be a smooth bounded domain in $\mathbb{R}^{n}\; (n \geq 2)$. For $m \geq 1$ an integer, suppose that $p \in W^{m,\infty}(Q)$, $h \in H^{m}(Q)$ satisfies $\partial^{k}_{t}h(\cdot, 0) = 0$ for integers $0 \leq k \leq m$, and $g \in H^{m + (3/2),m + (3/2)}(\Sigma)$ satisfies $\partial^{m}_{t}g(\cdot,0) = 0$ for integers $0 \leq k < m + (3/2)$. Then,
\begin{equation} \label{linear_equation_0}
\begin{cases}
(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p(x,t))v = h(x,t) \text{ on } Q,\\
v(x,t) = g(x,t) \text{ on } \Sigma,\\
v(x,0) = 0 \text{ on } \Omega \times \{0\}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
has a unique solution $v \in H^{m}(Q)$ satisfying the estimate
\begin{equation} \label{linear_equation_boundary_data_estimate}
\Vert v \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)} \leq C \left( \Vert g \Vert_{H^{m + (3/2), m + (3/2)}(\Sigma)} + \Vert h \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)}\right)
\end{equation}
for some constant $C = C\left(\Omega, T, \left\Vert p \right\Vert_{W^{m,\infty}(Q)} \right) > 0$. Moreover, we have that $\partial^{k}_{t}v(\cdot, 0) = 0$ for integers $0 \leq k \leq m$.

Using Theorem $2.3$ in Chapter $4$ of \cite{LM1972}, we have that there exists $v_{2} \in H^{m+2,m+2}(Q)$ such that
\begin{equation} \label{linear_equation_1_0}
\partial^{k}_{t}v_{2}(\cdot,0) = 0
\end{equation}
for integers $0 \leq k < m + (3/2)$ and that $g = v_{2}|_{\Sigma}$. The same theorem in tandem with the Bounded Inverse Theorem (e.g. Theorem $8.34$ in \cite{RR2004}) implies the inequality
\begin{equation} 
\Vert v_{2} \Vert_{H^{m+2,m + 2}(Q)} \leq C\Vert g \Vert_{H^{m + (3/2), m + (3/2)}(\Sigma)}
\end{equation}
for some constant $C = C(\Omega, T) > 0$. We also have from Proposition $2.3$ in Chapter $4$ of \cite{LM1972} the fact that
\begin{equation} \label{v_2_estimate_step_0}
\Vert v_{2} \Vert_{H^{m + 2}(Q)} \leq C\Vert v_{2} \Vert_{H^{m+2,m + 2}(Q)} \left( \leq C\Vert g \Vert_{H^{m + (3/2), m + (3/2)}(\Sigma)}\right)
\end{equation}
for some constant $C = C(\Omega, T) > 0$. Since $p \in W^{m,\infty}(Q)$, we have that $-(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p)v_{2} \in H^{m}(Q)$. We will now consider the solution $v_{1}(x,t)$ to
\begin{equation} \label{linear_equation_1}
\begin{cases}
(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p(x,t))v_{1} = F(x,t) \coloneqq h(x,t) - (i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p)v_{2} \text{ on } Q,\\
v_{1}(x,t) = 0 \text{ on } \Sigma,\\
v_{1}(x,0) = 0 \text{ on } \Omega \times \{0\}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
and show that $v \coloneqq v_{1} + v_{2}$ satisfies the estimate in \eqref{linear_equation_boundary_data_estimate}. \\ \indent
From Lemma \ref{KS2019_Lemma_2.2_statement}, we know that \eqref{linear_equation_1} has a unique solution in $L^{\infty}(0, T; L^{2}(\Omega))$ satisfying the estimate
\begin{equation} \label{linear_equation_1_estimates}
\Vert v_{1}\Vert_{L^{\infty}(0, T; L^{2}(\Omega))} \leq C\Vert F \Vert_{L^{2}(Q)}
\end{equation}
for some constant $C = C\left(\Omega, T, \left\Vert p \right\Vert_{W^{1,\infty}(Q)}\right) > 0$ provided that $F(\cdot,0) = 0$. In fact, recall that we have assumed $\partial^{k}_{t}h(\cdot, 0) = 0$ for integers $0 \leq k \leq m$. So, it follows from \eqref{linear_equation_1_0} that $\partial^{k}_{t}F(\cdot,0) = 0$ for integers $0 \leq k \leq m$. \\ \indent
We will now show that
\begin{equation} \label{v_1_estimate_step_0}
\begin{split}
\left\Vert \partial^{\alpha}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x}v_{1} \right\Vert_{L^{2}(Q)} \leq C\left\Vert F \right\Vert_{H^{m}(Q)}\text{ for } 0 \leq \alpha + |\beta| \leq m,\\
\partial^{\alpha}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x}v_{1}(\cdot, 0) = 0 \text{ for } 0 \leq \alpha + |\beta| \leq m,
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\beta$ is a multi-index and $C = C(\Omega, T, \left\Vert p \right\Vert_{W^{m,\infty}(Q)}) > 0$. Indeed, \eqref{v_1_estimate_step_0} holds when $\alpha + |\beta| = 0$ by the estimate in \eqref{linear_equation_1_estimates} and the initial data in \eqref{linear_equation_1}. Seeking to apply strong induction, suppose that the statements in \eqref{v_1_estimate_step_0} hold for $0 \leq \alpha + |\beta| \leq m-1$. By differentiating \eqref{linear_equation_1} and rearranging terms, we have that when $\alpha + |\beta| = m$,
\begin{equation} \label{v_1_estimate_step_1_1}
\partial^{\alpha}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x}v_{1} = i\Delta\left(\partial^{\alpha-1}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x} \right)v_{1} + i\left(\partial^{\alpha-1}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x} \right)(pv_{1}) -i \partial^{\alpha-1}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x} F.
\end{equation}
Thus, the induction hypothesis implies that $\partial^{\alpha}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x}v_{1}(\cdot, 0) = 0$. Again considering the equation obtained by differentiating \eqref{linear_equation_1}, we have (with $\alpha + |\beta| = m$) that
\begin{equation} \label{v_1_estimate_step_1}
(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p)\left(\partial^{\alpha}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x}v_{1} \right) = \\
\partial^{\alpha}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x}F + \sum_{\alpha_{0} + |\beta_{0}| = 0}^{m-1} C(\alpha_{0}, \beta_{0})\left(\partial^{\alpha - \alpha_{0}}_{t}\partial^{\beta - \beta_{0}}_{x}p\right)\partial^{\alpha_{0}}_{t}\partial^{\beta_{0}}_{x}v_{1}.
\end{equation}
The claimed estimate in \eqref{v_1_estimate_step_0} now follows from \eqref{linear_equation_1_estimates} and the induction hypothesis. We conclude that 
\begin{equation} \label{v_2_estimate_step_2}
\Vert v_{1} \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)} \leq C \Vert F \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)}
\end{equation}
for a constant $C = C(\Omega, T, \Vert p \Vert_{W^{m,\infty}(Q)}) > 0$, which combined with \eqref{v_2_estimate_step_0} yields \eqref{linear_equation_boundary_data_estimate}. The fact that $\partial^{k}_{t}v(\cdot, 0) = 0$ for integers $0 \leq k \leq m$ is a result of \eqref{linear_equation_1_0} and \eqref{v_1_estimate_step_0}.

\label{nonlinear_well-posedness}
Let $\Omega$ be a smooth bounded domain in $\mathbb{R}^{n}\; (n = 2, 3)$ and let $j\in \{1,2\}$. In \eqref{two_component_GPEqn}, suppose that $p_{j} \in W^{3,\infty}(Q)$ and that $q_{j}, \tilde{q}_{j} \in H^{3}(Q)$. Then, there exists $r \in (0,1)$ such that whenever $(g_{1},g_{2}) \in \left(\mathcal{B}_{r}(0)\right)^{2}$, there is a unique solution $(u_{1},u_{2}) \in \left(H^{3}(Q)\right)^{2}$ to \eqref{two_component_GPEqn} satisfying
\begin{equation} \label{nonlinear_equation_boundary_data_estimate}
\Vert u_{j}\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \leq C \Vert g_{j}\Vert_{H^{9/2,9/2}(\Sigma)}
\end{equation}
for some $C > 0$. The constants $C$ and $r$ depend on $\Omega, T, \left\Vert p_{j} \right\Vert_{W^{3,\infty}(Q)}, \left\Vert q_{j} \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}, \left\Vert \tilde{q}_{j} \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}$. Moreover, we have that $\partial^{k}_{t}u_{j}(\cdot, 0) = 0$ for $0 \leq k \leq 3$.

The proof relies on a standard argument based on the Contraction Mapping Principle. We refer the readers to \cite{AZ2025, LLZ2023, LUY2024, LOST2022} for related examples.\\ \\
\textit{Step 1 (A Fixed Point Equation).}\\ \indent
We seek to show that there is a solution of the form $(u_{1}, u_{2}) \coloneqq (\tilde{u}_{1} + w_{1}, \tilde{u}_{2} + w_{2})$ where $\tilde{u}_{j}$ satisfies
\begin{equation} \label{boundary_part_0}
\begin{cases}
\mathcal{T}_{j} \tilde{u}_{j} = 0 \text{ on } Q,\\
\tilde{u}_{j}(x,t) = g_{j}(x,t) \text{ on } \Sigma,\\
\tilde{u}_{j}(x,0) = 0 \text{ on } \Omega \times \{0\}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
and $w_{j}$ satisfies
\begin{equation} \label{source_part_0}
\begin{cases}
\mathcal{T}_{j} w_{j}  = f_{j}(u_{1},u_{2}) \coloneqq -q_{j}(x,t)\left|\tilde{u}_{j} + w_{j}\right|^{2}(\tilde{u}_{j} + w_{j})\\
-\; \tilde{q}_{j}(x, t)|\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + w_{\iota(j)}|^{2}(\tilde{u}_{j} + w_{j}) \text{ on } Q,\\
w_{j}(x,t) = 0 \text{ on } \Sigma,\\
w_{j}(x,0) = 0 \text{ on } \Omega \times \{0\}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
For $g_{j} \in \mathcal{H}^{9/2,9/2}(\Sigma)$ (recall that this space was defined in \eqref{boundary_neighborhood_0}), Theorem \ref{linear_well-posedness} (with $m = 3$) implies that \eqref{boundary_part_0} has a unique solution in $H^{3}(Q)$ satisfying the estimate
\begin{equation} \label{boundary_part_0_Theorem_2.2_estimate}
\Vert \tilde{u}_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \leq C \Vert g_{j} \Vert_{H^{9/2,9/2}(\Sigma)}
\end{equation}
for a constant $C = C\left(\Omega, T, \Vert p_{j} \Vert_{W^{3,\infty}(Q)}\right) > 0$. Moreover, we have that
\begin{equation} \label{boundary_part_0_vanishing_derivatives}
\partial^{k}_{t}\tilde{u}_{j}(\cdot,0) = 0 \quad (0 \leq k \leq 3). 
\end{equation}
Suppose also that
\begin{equation} \label{source_neighborhood_-1}
w_{j} \in \mathcal{H}^{3}(Q) \coloneqq \left\{w \in H^{3}(Q) \,\big|\, \partial^{k}_{t}w(\cdot, 0) = 0 \text{ for } 0 \leq k \leq 3 \right\}.
\end{equation}
With reference to the source term in \eqref{source_part_0}, one then has that
\begin{equation} \label{source_neighborhood_1}
\partial_{t}^{k}f_{j}(u_{1}(\cdot, 0), u_{2}(\cdot, 0)) = 0 \quad (0 \leq k \leq 3)
\end{equation}
and as a result, we can apply the results of Theorem \ref{linear_well-posedness} to \eqref{source_part_0}. In particular, we have the estimate
\begin{equation} \label{source_part_0_Theorem_2.2_estimate}
\Vert w_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \leq C\Vert f_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}
\end{equation}
for a constant $C = C\left(\Omega, T, \Vert p_{j} \Vert_{W^{3,\infty}(Q)}\right) > 0$. \\ \indent
For $r > 0$, define the neighborhood
\begin{equation} \label{source_neighborhood}
\mathcal{C}_{r}(0) \coloneqq \mathcal{H}^{3}(Q) \cap \left\{g \in H^{3}(Q) \,\big|\, \Vert g \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \leq r\right\}.
\end{equation}
For some $g_{j} \in \mathcal{B}_{r}(0)$ that is fixed and $w_{j} \in \mathcal{C}_{r}(0)$ (where $r > 0$ will be fixed later), consider the unique solution to \eqref{source_part_0}, which we denote by $S_{j}(u_{1}, u_{2}) \coloneqq S(f_{j}(u_{1},u_{2})) \in H^{3}(Q)$. We seek to find a fixed point $(w_{1},w_{2})$ such that
\begin{equation} \label{fixed_point_condition_0}
(w_{1},w_{2}) = S(\tilde{u}_{1} + w_{1}, \tilde{u}_{2} + w_{2}) \coloneqq (S_{1}(\tilde{u}_{1} + w_{1}, \tilde{u}_{2} + w_{2}), S_{2}(\tilde{u}_{1} + w_{1}, \tilde{u}_{2} + w_{2}))
\end{equation}
We will show that \eqref{fixed_point_condition_0} has a fixed point by showing that the solution map
\begin{equation} \label{contraction_map_candidate}
(w_{1},w_{2}) \longmapsto S(\tilde{u}_{1} + w_{1}, \tilde{u}_{2} + w_{2})
\end{equation}
is a contraction map on $(\mathcal{C}_{r}(0))^{2}$ when $r > 0$ is sufficiently small and $g_{j} \in \mathcal{B}_{r}(0)$. \\ \\
\textit{Step 2 (Closure of the Solution Map).}\\ \indent
We begin by checking that if $(g_{1},g_{2}) \in (\mathcal{B}_{r}(0))^{2}$ and $(w_{1}, w_{2})\in (\mathcal{C}_{r}(0))^{2}$ for $r > 0$ sufficiently small, then $S(f_{1},f_{2}) \in (\mathcal{C}_{r}(0))^{2}$. Indeed, as a result of the estimates in \eqref{boundary_part_0_Theorem_2.2_estimate} and \eqref{source_part_0_Theorem_2.2_estimate} along with the fact that $H^{3}(Q)$ is a Banach algebra, we have
\begin{equation} \label{closure_verification}
\Vert S_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \leq C\Vert |\tilde{u}_{j} + w_{j}|^{2}(\tilde{u}_{j} + w_{j}) + |\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + w_{\iota(j)}|^{2}(\tilde{u}_{j} + w_{j}) \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \leq Cr^{3} \leq r
\end{equation}
for an appropriate choice of $r \in (0, 1)$.\\ \\
\textit{Step 3 (Verifying the Contraction Mapping Property).}\\ \indent
Now, we will show that if $r \in (0, 1)$ is sufficiently small and
\begin{equation} \label{contraction_verification_0_0_0}
\begin{split}
(g_{1},g_{2}) \in (\mathcal{B}_{r}(0))^{2},\\
(w_{1},w_{2}),(W_{1},W_{2}) \in (\mathcal{C}_{r}(0))^{2},
\end{split}
\end{equation}
then there exists $C \in (0, 1)$ such that
\begin{equation} \label{contraction_verification_0_0}
\begin{split}
\Vert S(\tilde{u}_{1} + w_{1}, \tilde{u}_{2} + w_{2}) - S(\tilde{u}_{1} + W_{1}, \tilde{u}_{2} + W_{2}) \Vert_{\left(H^{3}(Q)\right)^{2}}\\
\leq C\left(\left\Vert (w_{1}, w_{2}) - (W_{1}, W_{2}) \right\Vert_{\left(H^{3}(Q)\right)^{2}} \right).
\end{split}
\end{equation}
When we apply Theorem \ref{linear_well-posedness} to \eqref{source_part_0}, we obtain the following estimate for each component of the solution map:
\begin{equation} \label{contraction_verification_0}
\begin{split}
\Vert S_{j}(\tilde{u}_{1} + w_{1}, \tilde{u}_{2} + w_{2}) - S_{j}(\tilde{u}_{1} + W_{1}, \tilde{u}_{2} + W_{2}) \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}\\
\leq C \Vert f_{j}(\tilde{u}_{1} + w_{1}, \tilde{u}_{2} + w_{2}) - f_{j}(\tilde{u}_{1} + W_{1}, \tilde{u}_{2} + W_{2})  \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
To further develop the estimate in \eqref{contraction_verification_0}, we recall the definition of $f_{j}$ in \eqref{source_part_0} and note the following for the coupled source term:
\begin{equation} \label{contraction_verification_1}
\begin{split}
\left\Vert |\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + w_{\iota(j)}|^{2}(\tilde{u}_{j} + w_{j}) - |\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + W_{\iota(j)}|^{2}(\tilde{u}_{j} + W_{j})  \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \\
\leq \left\Vert |\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + w_{\iota(j)}|^{2}(\tilde{u}_{j} + w_{j}) - |\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + w_{\iota(j)}|^{2}(\tilde{u}_{j} + W_{j})  \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}\\
+\; \left\Vert |\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + w_{\iota(j)}|^{2}(\tilde{u}_{j} + W_{j}) - |\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + W_{\iota(j)}|^{2}(\tilde{u}_{j} + W_{j})  \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}\\
\leq Cr^{2} \left\Vert w_{j} - W_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} + Cr \Vert |\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + w_{\iota(j)}|^{2} - |\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + W_{\iota(j)}|^{2} \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
In \eqref{contraction_verification_1}, we used the Banach algebra property of $H^{3}(Q)$ and the assumptions in \eqref{contraction_verification_0_0_0}. Now, by adding and subtracting $(\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + w_{\iota(j)})(\overline{\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + W_{\iota(j)}})$ inside the last term on the right-hand side of \eqref{contraction_verification_1}, one can show that
\begin{equation} \label{contraction_verification_2}
\left\Vert |\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + w_{\iota(j)}|^{2} - |\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + W_{\iota(j)}|^{2}  \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \leq Cr \left\Vert w_{\iota(j)} - W_{\iota(j)} \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}.
\end{equation}
Taking $r \in (0, 1)$ sufficiently small and similarly estimating the terms in \eqref{contraction_verification_0} resulting from the uncoupled source term in \eqref{source_part_0}, the claimed inequality in \eqref{contraction_verification_0_0} follows.\\ \indent
So, the Contraction Mapping Principle implies that there is a unique fixed point $(w_{1}, w_{2}) \in (\mathcal{C}_{r}(0))^{2}$ satisfying \eqref{fixed_point_condition_0} and thus a unique solution of the form $(u_{1}, u_{2}) \coloneqq (\tilde{u}_{1} + w_{1}, \tilde{u}_{2} + w_{2})$ to \eqref{two_component_GPEqn}. From equations \eqref{boundary_part_0_vanishing_derivatives} and \eqref{source_neighborhood_-1} regarding the vanishing derivatives of $\tilde{u}_{j}$ and $w_{j}$, we have the fact that $\partial^{k}_{t}u_{j}(\cdot, 0) = 0$ for $0 \leq k \leq 3$.\\ \\
\textit{Step 4 (Proof of the Stability Estimate).}\\ \indent
From the first inequality in \eqref{closure_verification} and the assumptions that
\begin{equation} \label{stability_estimate_0_1}
\begin{split}
(g_{1},g_{2}) \in (\mathcal{B}_{r}(0))^{2},\\
(w_{1}, w_{2})\in (\mathcal{C}_{r}(0))^{2}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
we can write
\begin{equation}\label{stability_estimate_0}
\Vert w_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} = \Vert S_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \leq Cr^{2}\left(\Vert \tilde{u}_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} + \Vert w_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}\right).
\end{equation}
After taking $r \in (0, 1)$ sufficiently small and applying \eqref{boundary_part_0_Theorem_2.2_estimate} to estimate $\Vert \tilde{u}_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}$, equation \eqref{stability_estimate_0} becomes
\begin{equation} \label{stability_estimate_1}
\Vert w_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \leq C\Vert g_{j} \Vert_{H^{9/2,9/2}(\Sigma)}.
\end{equation}
Now, the fact that $u_{j} \coloneqq \tilde{u}_{j} + w_{j}$ yields the stability estimate in \eqref{nonlinear_equation_boundary_data_estimate}. We see from Theorem \ref{linear_well-posedness} and the use of the Banach algebra property to absorb contributions from $\left\Vert q_{j} \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}, \left\Vert \tilde{q}_{j} \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}$ that the constants $C, r$ depend on the parameters $\Omega, T, \left\Vert p_{j} \right\Vert_{W^{3,\infty}(Q)}, \left\Vert q_{j} \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}, \left\Vert \tilde{q}_{j} \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}$.

\label{stability_estimate_general_coupled_system}
The estimate \eqref{nonlinear_equation_boundary_data_estimate} is a special case of the more general inequality
\begin{equation} \label{stability_estimate_0_0_alternative_term}
\Vert (u_{1}, u_{2}) \Vert_{\left(H^{3}(Q)\right)^{2}} \leq C\Vert (g_{1}, g_{2}) \Vert_{\left(H^{3}(Q)\right)^{2}}.
\end{equation}
That the stability estimate \eqref{nonlinear_equation_boundary_data_estimate} involves only $g_{j}$ rather than $(g_{1}, g_{2})$ is a consequence of the structure of the nonlinear term $f_{j}$ in \eqref{source_part_0}. In a general coupled system, both $g_{1}$ and $g_{2}$ contribute to the solution. As an example, suppose that we instead considered a system where
\begin{equation} \label{alternative_nonlinear_term}
f_{j}(u_{1}, u_{2}) \coloneqq -q_{j}|u_{j}|^{2}u_{j} + \tilde{q}_{j}u_{\iota(j)}^{3}.
\end{equation}
We will now replace the nonlinearity in \eqref{two_component_GPEqn_small_data} with the one defined by \eqref{alternative_nonlinear_term} and try to prepare an estimate similar to \eqref{stability_estimate_0}. We begin with an estimate similar to \eqref{closure_verification}, namely
\begin{equation} \label{stability_estimate_0_alternative_term}
\begin{split}
\Vert w_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} = \Vert S_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}
\leq C\Vert |\tilde{u}_{j} + w_{j}|^{2}(\tilde{u}_{j} + w_{j}) + (\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + w_{\iota(j)})^{3} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Now, we seek to factor $r^{2}$ as in \eqref{stability_estimate_0} using the assumptions $(g_{1}, g_{2}) \in (\mathcal{B}_{r}(0))^{2}$ and $(w_{1}, w_{2}) \in (\mathcal{C}_{r}(0))^{2}$. This yields
\begin{equation} \label{stability_estimate_1_alternative_term}
\begin{split}
\Vert w_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \leq Cr^{2}\left(\Vert \tilde{u}_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} + \Vert w_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} + \Vert \tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} + \Vert w_{\iota(j)} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}\right), 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where we majorized the terms $|\tilde{u}_{j} + w_{j}|^{2}$ and $(\tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} + w_{\iota(j)})^{2}$ from the right-hand side of \eqref{stability_estimate_0_alternative_term} by a constant multiple of $r^{2}$. Applying Theorem \ref{linear_well-posedness} to $\Vert \tilde{u}_{j} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}$ and $\Vert \tilde{u}_{\iota(j)} \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}$, we see from \eqref{stability_estimate_1_alternative_term} that both $g_{1}$ and $g_{2}$ contribute to the solution in this case. Repeating the argument yields the same estimate for $\Vert w_{\iota(j)}\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}$. So, adding the estimates for $\Vert\tilde{u}_{j} + w_{j}\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}\;(j = 1,2)$ yields \eqref{stability_estimate_0_0_alternative_term}.

\label{rigorous_linearization_lemma}
Suppose that $p_{j} \in W^{3,\infty}(Q)$ and the nonlinear coefficients in \eqref{two_component_GPEqn_small_data} satisfy \eqref{coefficient_smallness_assumption}. Recalling $\mathcal{B}_{r}(0)$ (defined in \eqref{boundary_neighborhood}) for the $r > 0$ defined by Theorem \ref{nonlinear_well-posedness}, suppose that the nonlinear equation \eqref{two_component_GPEqn_small_data} is equipped with boundary data
\begin{equation}
\epsilon_{1}f^{(j)}_{1} + \epsilon_{2}f^{(j)}_{2} + \epsilon_{3}f^{(j)}_{3} \in \mathcal{B}_{r}(0),
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon_{l} \in \mathbb{R}\;(l = 1,2,3)$ is small enough to ensure that \eqref{two_component_GPEqn_small_data} has a unique solution in $H^{3}(Q)$ satisfying
\begin{equation} \label{two_component_GP_eqn_small_data_vanishing_derivatives}
\partial^{k}_{t}u_{j,k}(\cdot, 0) = 0 \quad (0 \leq k \leq 3)
\end{equation}
and the estimate \eqref{nonlinear_equation_boundary_data_estimate}. Then, the solution to \eqref{two_component_GPEqn_small_data} can be written in the following form:
\begin{equation} \label{nonlinear_solution_Taylor_series_0}
u_{j,k}(x,t) = \mathcal{L}_{j}(x,t) + \frac{1}{6}\left(\sum_{(\alpha,\beta,\gamma) \in \{1,2,3\}^{3}} \epsilon_{\alpha} \epsilon_{\beta} \epsilon_{\gamma} w^{(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)}_{j,k}(x,t)\right) + \mathcal{R}_{j,k}(x,t),
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{R}_{j,k}$ satisfies the estimate
\begin{equation}\label{Taylor_series_remainder_estimate_0}
\left \Vert \mathcal{R}_{j,k}\right \Vert_{H^{3}(Q)}
\leq C(\left\vert\epsilon_{1}\right\vert + \left\vert\epsilon_{2}\right\vert + \left\vert\epsilon_{3}\right\vert)^{5} \cdot 
\left[\sum_{l = 1}^{3}\left \Vert f^{(j)}_{l} \right \Vert_{H^{9/2,9/2}(\Sigma)}  \right]^{5}
\end{equation}
for some constant $C = C\left(\Omega, T, \left\Vert p_{j} \right\Vert_{W^{3,\infty}(Q)}, M_{j}\right) > 0$.

From \eqref{first_order_linearization_time-dependent}-\eqref{kth_order_linearization_time-dependent}, we have that if \eqref{nonlinear_solution_Taylor_series_0} holds, then $R_{j,k}$ satisfies
\begin{equation} \label{Taylor_series_remainder_equation}
\begin{cases}
\mathcal{T}_{j}R_{j,k} = -q_{j,k}\left(|u_{j,k}|^{2}u_{j,k} - \left|\mathcal{L}_{j}\right|^{2}\mathcal{L}_{j} \right) -\; \tilde{q}_{j,k}\left(|u_{\iota(j),k}|^{2}u_{j,k} - \left|\mathcal{L}_{\iota(j)}\right|^{2}\mathcal{L}_{j} \right) \text{ on } Q,\\
R_{j,k}(x,t) = 0 \text{ on } \Sigma,\\
R_{j,k}(x,0) = 0 \text{ on } \Omega \times \{0\}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
This a result of the fact that
\begin{equation} \label{Taylor_series_remainder_equation_1}
\frac{1}{6}\left(\sum_{(\alpha,\beta,\gamma) \in \{1,2,3\}^{3}} \epsilon_{\alpha} \epsilon_{\beta} \epsilon_{\gamma} \left(\mathcal{S}^{(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)}_{j,k} + \tilde{\mathcal{S}}^{(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)}_{j,k} \right)\right) = -q_{j,k}\left|\mathcal{L}_{j} \right|^{2}\mathcal{L}_{j} - \tilde{q}_{j,k}\left|\mathcal{L}_{\iota(j)} \right|^{2}\mathcal{L}_{j},
\end{equation}
which can be checked by a direct calculation. Since $q_{j,k}, \tilde{q}_{j,k}, u_{j,k}, \mathcal{L}_{j} \in H^{3}(Q)$, the Banach algebra property of $H^{3}(Q)$ implies that the source term in \eqref{Taylor_series_remainder_equation} belongs to $H^{3}(Q)$. In view of \eqref{first_order_linearization_vanishing_derivatives} and \eqref{two_component_GP_eqn_small_data_vanishing_derivatives}, \eqref{Taylor_series_remainder_equation} has a unique solution in $H^{3}(Q)$ by Theorem \ref{linear_well-posedness}. \\ \indent
The calculation leading to \eqref{Taylor_series_remainder_estimate_0} makes use of the fact that $u_{j,k} - \mathcal{L}_{j}$ satisfies \eqref{two_component_GPEqn_small_data} but with trivial initial and boundary data. Then, for example in the first source term in \eqref{Taylor_series_remainder_equation}, we can add and subtract terms to obtain
\begin{equation} \label{solution_series_remainder_equation_-1}
\begin{split}
-q_{j,k}\left(|u_{j,k}|^{2}u_{j,k} - \left|\mathcal{L}_{j}\right|^{2}\mathcal{L}_{j}\right) = -q_{j,k}\Big(|u_{j,k}|^{2}\left(u_{j,k} - \mathcal{L}_{j} \right)\\
+\;  \mathcal{L}_{j}u_{j,k}\left(\overline{u_{j,k}} - \mathcal{L}_{j}\right) + \left(\mathcal{L}_{j}\right)^{2}\left( u_{j,k} - \overline{\mathcal{L}_{j}} \right)\Big).
\end{split}
\end{equation}
The estimates from Theorem \ref{linear_well-posedness}-\ref{nonlinear_well-posedness} say that
\begin{equation} \label{solution_series_remainder_equation_0}
\begin{split}
\left\Vert u_{j,k} \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \leq C\sum_{l = 1}^{3}\left|\epsilon_{l}\right|\left \Vert f^{(j)}_{l} \right \Vert_{H^{9/2,9/2}(\Sigma)},\\
\left\Vert \mathcal{L}_{j} \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \leq C\sum_{l = 1}^{3}\left|\epsilon_{l}\right|\left \Vert f^{(j)}_{l} \right \Vert_{H^{9/2,9/2}(\Sigma)},\\
\left\Vert u_{j,k} - \mathcal{L}_{j} \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \leq C\left(\left\Vert u_{j,k} \right\Vert^{3}_{H^{3}(Q)} + \left\Vert u_{\iota(j),k} \right\Vert^{2}_{H^{3}(Q)}\left\Vert u_{j,k} \right\Vert_{H^{3}(Q)} \right)
\end{split}
\end{equation}
for a constant $C = C\left(\Omega, T, \left\Vert p_{j} \right\Vert_{W^{3,\infty}(Q)}, M_{j}\right) > 0$. The Banach algebra property of $H^{3}(Q)$ was applied to obtain the last inequality in \eqref{solution_series_remainder_equation_0}. We also used \eqref{coefficient_smallness_assumption} to absorb the contribution of the nonlinear coefficient $q_{j,k}$ into the constant. If we use \eqref{solution_series_remainder_equation_0} to estimate the right-hand side of \eqref{solution_series_remainder_equation_-1} and also treat the second source term in \eqref{Taylor_series_remainder_equation} similarly, we obtain the inequality in \eqref{Taylor_series_remainder_estimate_0}.

\label{nonlinear_solution_Taylor_series_corollary}
Suppose that the conditions of Lemma \ref{rigorous_linearization_lemma} are satisfied. Then, we have that
\begin{equation}
v^{(l)}_{j} = \partial_{\epsilon_{l}}u_{j,k}\big|_{\epsilon_{l} = 0} \quad \text{and} \quad w^{(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)}_{j,k} = \partial^{3}_{\epsilon_{\alpha}\epsilon_{\beta}\epsilon_{\gamma}}u_{j,k}\big|_{\epsilon_{\alpha} = \epsilon_{\beta} = \epsilon_{\gamma} = 0},
\end{equation}
where the partial derivatives are taken in the sense of the mapping from the boundary data $\epsilon_{1}f^{(j)}_{1} + \epsilon_{2}f^{(j)}_{2} + \epsilon_{3}f^{(j)}_{3} \in \mathcal{B}_{r}(0)$ to the unique solution of \eqref{two_component_GPEqn_small_data} in $H^{3}(Q)$.

\label{linear_coefficient_recovery}
In view of Corollary \ref{nonlinear_solution_Taylor_series_corollary}, one can apply Theorem $1.1$ from \cite{BB2020} to \eqref{first_order_linearization_time-dependent} in order to show that the linearized Dirichlet-to-Neumann map defined by
\begin{equation}
f^{(j)}_{l} \in \mathcal{B}_{r}(0) \longmapsto \partial_{\nu}v^{(l)}_{j}\big|_{\Sigma_{j}}
\end{equation}
stably determines the coefficient $p_{j}$.

\label{main_integral_identity}
Recalling the functions $\mathcal{S}^{(1,2,3)}_{j,k}, \tilde{\mathcal{S}}^{(1,2,3)}_{j,k}, w^{(1,2,3)}_{j,k} \in H^{3}(Q)$ defined in \eqref{kth_order_linearization_time-dependent}-\eqref{kth_order_time-dependent_source_terms}, consider
\begin{equation} \label{integral_identity_0_0}
\begin{split}
\mathcal{S}^{(1,2,3)}_{j} \coloneqq \mathcal{S}^{(1,2,3)}_{j,1} - \mathcal{S}^{(1,2,3)}_{j,2},\,
\tilde{\mathcal{S}}^{(1,2,3)}_{j} \coloneqq \tilde{\mathcal{S}}^{(1,2,3)}_{j,1} - \tilde{\mathcal{S}}^{(1,2,3)}_{j,2},\\
w^{(1,2,3)}_{j} \coloneqq w^{(1,2,3)}_{j,1} - w^{(1,2,3)}_{j,2}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
We have that\footnote{Note that $\left[\Delta, \chi \right] = \Delta \chi + 2(\nabla \chi \cdot \nabla)$.}
\begin{equation} \label{integral_identity_0}
\int_{0}^{T^{*}}\int_{\Omega} \left[\chi\left(\mathcal{S}^{(1,2,3)}_{j} + \tilde{\mathcal{S}}^{(1,2,3)}_{j} \right) + \left(\left[\Delta,\chi \right]w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\right)\right]\overline{v_{0,j}}(x,t) \,dx\,dt = 0.
\end{equation}

The function $\chi(x)w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}(x,t)$ satisfies \eqref{kth_order_linearization_time-dependent} with source term
\begin{equation} \label{time_dependent_kth-order_cut-off_source_term}
\chi\left(\mathcal{S}^{(1,2,3)}_{j} + \tilde{\mathcal{S}}^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\right)(x,t) + \left(\left[\Delta,\chi \right]w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\right)(x,t).
\end{equation}
Let us now multiply both sides of the equation satisfied by $\chi w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}$ by $\overline{v_{0,j}}$ and integrate both sides over $\Omega \times (0, T^{*})$. Integrating by parts on the resulting left-hand side, we obtain
\begin{equation} \label{integral_identity_1}
\begin{split}
\int_{\Omega \times (0, T^{*})} \left[(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p_{j})\left(\chi w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\right)\right]\overline{v_{0,j}}(x,t)\,dx\,dt \\
 = i\int_{0}^{T^{*}}\int_{\Omega} \partial_{t}\left(\chi w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\overline{v_{0,j}}\right) \,dx\,dt - i\int_{0}^{T^{*}}\int_{\Omega} \chi w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\left(\partial_{t}\overline{v_{0,j}}\right)(x,t)\,dx\,dt \\
 +\;\int_{0}^{T^{*}}\int_{\partial \Omega} \partial_{\nu}\left(\chi w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\right)\overline{v_{0,j}}(x,t)\,dS\,dt - \int_{0}^{T^{*}}\int_{\Omega} \left[\nabla\left(\chi w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\right) \cdot \nabla \overline{v_{0,j}}\right](x,t)\,dx\,dt\\
 +\;\int_{0}^{T^{*}}\int_{\Omega} \chi w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\left(p_{j} \overline{v_{0,j}}\right)(x,t)\,dx\,dt.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
The boundary integral in \eqref{integral_identity_1} vanishes since $w^{(1,2,3)}_{j} \equiv 0$ on $\mathcal{O}^{(j)} \times (0, T^{*})$ (owing to the fact that $q_{j,1}-q_{j,2} \equiv \tilde{q}_{j,1} - \tilde{q}_{j,2} \equiv 0$ on $\mathcal{O}^{(j)} \times (0, T^{*})$ from the assumption in \eqref{near_the_boundary_a_priori_assumption}).\\ \indent
Again integrating by parts, note that
\begin{equation} \label{integral_identity_2}
\begin{split}
\int_{0}^{T^{*}} \left[\nabla\left(\chi w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\right) \cdot \nabla \overline{v_{0,j}}\right](x,t)\,dx\,dt = \int_{0}^{T^{*}}\int_{\partial \Omega} \chi w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\left(\partial_{\nu}\overline{v_{0,j}} \right)(x,t)\,dS\,dt\\
\;- \int_{0}^{T^{*}}\int_{\Omega} \chi w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\left(\Delta\overline{v_{0,j}}\right)\,dx\,dt.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
The boundary integral in \eqref{integral_identity_2} vanishes again using the fact that $w^{(1,2,3)}_{j} \equiv 0$ on $\mathcal{O}^{(j)} \times (0, T^{*})$. Then, since $w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}(\cdot,0) \equiv 0$ and $v_{0,j}(\cdot,T^{*}) \equiv 0$, \eqref{integral_identity_1} becomes
\begin{equation} \label{integral_identity_3}
\begin{split}
\int_{\Omega \times (0, T^{*})} \left[(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p_{j})\left(\chi w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\right)\right]\overline{v_{0,j}}(x,t)\,dx\,dt\\
=\int_{\Omega \times (0, T^{*})} \left(\chi w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}\right)\left(-i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p_{j} \right)\overline{v_{0,j}}(x,t)\,dx\,dt.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
The right-hand side of \eqref{integral_identity_3} vanishes by \eqref{adjoint_problem_CGO_approach} assuming that $p_{j}$ is real-valued. As a result, we obtain the identity in \eqref{integral_identity_0}. We point out that the calculations in \eqref{integral_identity_1}-\eqref{integral_identity_3} are justified since we had taken $\mathcal{S}^{(1,2,3)}_{j}, \tilde{\mathcal{S}}^{(1,2,3)}_{j}, w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}, v_{0,j} \in H^{3}(Q)$.

\label{LLZ2023_CGO_estimate_proposition}
Fix an integer $N > 0$ in \eqref{CGO_solution_0}, an integer $m \geq 1$, and let $p \in W^{2N + m,\infty}(Q)$. Further consider a fixed vector $\omega \in \mathbb{R}^{n}$ along with fixed parameters $h \in (0, \min\{1,T^{*}/4\})$ and $\tau \in \mathbb{R}$. For $a_{0}(x,t) = \theta_{h}(t)$, we have the estimates
\begin{equation} \label{L_infty_estimates_1}
\begin{split}
\Vert a_{k'} \Vert_{L^{\infty}(Q)} \leq Ch^{-k'}\; (0 \leq k' \leq N),\\
\Vert r \Vert_{L^{\infty}(Q)} \leq C\rho^{-N+2}h^{-N-2}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation} \label{H_m_estimates_1}
\begin{split}
\Vert a_{k'} \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)} \leq \tilde{C}h^{-k'-m}\; (0 \leq k' \leq N),\\
\Vert r \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)} \leq \tilde{C}\rho^{-N+2m}h^{-N-m-1}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
For $a_{0}(x,t) = \theta_{h}(t)e^{i(t\tau + x \cdot \xi)}$, we have the estimates
\begin{equation} \label{L_infty_estimates_0}
\begin{split}
\Vert a_{k'} \Vert_{L^{\infty}(Q)} \leq C\left<\tau, \xi \right>^{2k'}h^{-k'}\; (0 \leq k' \leq N),\\
\Vert r \Vert_{L^{\infty}(Q)} \leq C \rho^{-N+2} \left<\tau,\xi \right>^{2N + 2}h^{-N-2}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation} \label{H_m_estimates_0}
\begin{split}
\Vert a_{k'} \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)} \leq \tilde{C} \left<\tau,\xi \right>^{2k'+m}h^{-k'-m}\; (0 \leq k' \leq N),\\
\Vert r \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)} \leq \tilde{C}\rho^{-N+2m} \left<\tau,\xi \right>^{2N + m + 2}h^{-N-m-1}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
For the $L^{\infty}(Q)$ estimates in \eqref{L_infty_estimates_1} and \eqref{L_infty_estimates_0}, we have $C = C\left(\Omega, T, \left\Vert p \right\Vert_{W^{2N + 1,\infty}(Q)} \right) > 0$. For the $H^{m}(Q)$ estimates in \eqref{H_m_estimates_1} and \eqref{H_m_estimates_0}, we have $\tilde{C} = \tilde{C}(\Omega, T, \left\Vert p \right\Vert_{W^{2N + m,\infty}(Q)}) > 0$.

Our primary tool will be the estimate of $\Vert \theta_{h} \Vert_{W^{j,\infty}(\mathbb{R})}$ provided in \eqref{leading_order_amplitude_estimate}.\\ \\
\textbf{(I)} We first consider $a_{0} = \theta_{h}(t)$ (whence $a_{1} = -(1/2)|\omega|^{-2}(\partial_{t}\theta_{h})(x \cdot \omega)$ from \eqref{non-adjoint_first_order_amplitude}). In this case, when $k' = 0$ or $1$, the amplitude estimate in \eqref{L_infty_estimates_1} follows directly from \eqref{leading_order_amplitude_estimate}. 





For $2 \leq k' \leq N$, it follows by \eqref{adjoint_non-adjoint_higher_order_amplitude} and induction that the highest order derivative of $p$ arises from $\Delta^{k'-2}p$ while the highest order (nonzero) derivative of $\theta_{h}$ arises from $(i\partial_{t})^{k'-1}a_{1}$. Indeed, if this is true for some $2 \leq k' \leq N$, \eqref{adjoint_non-adjoint_higher_order_amplitude} shows that in $a_{k' + 1}$, the highest order derivative of $p$ is due to $\Delta a_{k'-1}$, thus $\Delta\left(\Delta \right)^{k'-2}p$. Likewise, the highest order (nonzero) derivative of $\theta_{h}$ in $a_{k' + 1}$ is due to $(i\partial_{t})a_{k'-1}$, thus $(i\partial_{t})^{k'}a_{1}$.\\ \indent
Since $h \in (0, \min\{1, T^{*}/4\})$, it follows from \eqref{leading_order_amplitude_estimate} and the preceding derivative count that for $0 \leq k' \leq N$:
\begin{equation} \label{amplitude_estimate_1_step_1}
\begin{split}
\left\Vert a_{k'} \right\Vert_{L^{\infty}(Q)} \leq Ch^{-k'},\\
\left\Vert \partial^{\alpha}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x}a_{k'}\right\Vert_{L^{2}(Q)} \leq \tilde{C}h^{-k'-m}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
for $0 \leq \alpha + |\beta| \leq m$ and constants $C = C\left(\Omega, \Vert p \Vert_{W^{2(N-2),\infty}(Q)}\right) > 0, \tilde{C} = \tilde{C}\left(\Omega, \Vert p \Vert_{W^{2(N-2) + m,\infty}(Q)}\right) > 0$. This completes the proofs of the amplitude estimates in \eqref{L_infty_estimates_1}-\eqref{H_m_estimates_1}.\\ \indent
To prove the remainder estimates in \eqref{L_infty_estimates_1}-\eqref{H_m_estimates_1}, we apply Lemma \ref{KS2019_Lemma_2.2_statement} and Theorem \ref{linear_well-posedness} to \eqref{CGO_0_remainder_equation}, which (when combined with the Sobolev Embedding Theorem) yield the inequalities
\begin{equation} \label{remainder_estimate_1_step_0}
\begin{split}
\Vert r \Vert_{L^{\infty}(Q)} \leq C \Vert r \Vert_{C([0, T]; H^{2}(\Omega))} \leq C\rho^{-N}\Vert e^{i\Phi}(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p)a_{N} \Vert_{H^{1}(0, T; L^{2}(\Omega))},\\
\Vert r \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)} \leq C \rho^{-N} \Vert e^{i\Phi}(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p)a_{N} \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
for some constant $C = C\left(\Omega, T, \left\Vert p \right\Vert_{W^{m,\infty}(Q)} \right) > 0$. We point that \eqref{smooth_cut-off_function_1_observation} ensured that it was possible to apply Theorem \ref{linear_well-posedness} to \eqref{CGO_0_remainder_equation}. \\ \indent
By substituting \eqref{adjoint_non-adjoint_higher_order_amplitude} (with $k' = N$) into $\Vert e^{i\Phi}(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p)a_{N} \Vert_{H^{1}(0, T; L^{2}(\Omega))}$, we see that the highest order derivative of $p$ arises from $\partial_{t}(\Delta a_{N})$ (that is, $\partial_{t}\Delta(\Delta^{N - 2}p)$) while the highest order (nonzero) derivative of $\theta_{h}$ arises from $\partial_{t}\left(i\partial_{t}\left(i\partial_{t}\right)^{N-1}a_{1} \right)$. We also obtain a factor of $\rho^{2}$ from $\partial_{t}\left(e^{i\Phi} \right)$. \\ \indent Likewise, in $\Vert e^{i\Phi}(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p)a_{N} \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)}$, the highest order derivative of $p$ arises from $\partial^{\alpha}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x}(\Delta a_{N})\; (\alpha + |\beta| = m)$ (thus from $\partial^{\alpha}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x}\Delta(\Delta^{N - 2}p)\; (\alpha + |\beta| = m)$) while the highest order (nonzero) derivative of $\theta_{h}$ arises from $\partial^{m}_{t}\left(i\partial_{t}\left(i\partial_{t}\right)^{N-1}a_{1} \right)$. We obtain a factor of $\rho^{2m}$ from $\partial^{m}_{t}\left(e^{\Phi}\right)$. \\ \indent
These considerations, along with \eqref{leading_order_amplitude_estimate} and the fact that $h \in (0, \min\{1, T^{*}/4\})$, yield the estimates
\begin{equation} \label{remainder_estimate_1_step_1}
\begin{split}
\Vert r \Vert_{L^{\infty}(Q)} \leq C\rho^{-N+2}h^{-(N+2)},\\
\Vert r \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)} \leq \tilde{C}\rho^{-N+2m}h^{-(N + m + 1)}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
for constants $C = C\left(\Omega, T, \Vert p \Vert_{W^{2N - 1, \infty}(Q)}\right) > 0, \tilde{C} = \tilde{C}\left(\Omega, T, \Vert p \Vert_{W^{2(N-1) + m, \infty}(Q)} \right) > 0$. \\ \\
\textbf{(II)} The proofs of \eqref{L_infty_estimates_0}-\eqref{H_m_estimates_0} when $a_{0} = \theta_{h}(t)e^{i(t\tau + x \cdot \xi)}$ are similar except that we obtain factors of $\left<\tau, \xi \right>$ from the exponential term. In what follows, we discuss the proofs of the $\Vert r \Vert_{L^{\infty}(Q)}$ and $\Vert r \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)}$ estimates in \eqref{L_infty_estimates_0}-\eqref{H_m_estimates_0}. \\ \indent                                                                                             




Let us apply \eqref{adjoint_non-adjoint_higher_order_amplitude} (with $k' = N$ in the case of $a_{0} = \theta_{h}(t)e^{i(t\tau + x \cdot \xi)}$) in the first and second inequalities listed in \eqref{remainder_estimate_1_step_0}. We observe that we obtain factors of $\rho^{2}, \rho^{2m}$ due to $\partial_{t}\left(e^{i\Phi}\right), \partial^{m}_{t}\left(e^{i\Phi}\right)$ in the first and second inequalities from \eqref{remainder_estimate_1_step_0} respectively. In $\Vert e^{i\Phi}(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p)a_{N} \Vert_{H^{1}(0, T; L^{2}(\Omega))}$, we have that the highest order derivative of $p$ arises from $\partial_{t}(\Delta a_{N})$ (thus from $\partial_{t}\Delta(\Delta^{N-1}p)$ by inductively applying \eqref{adjoint_non-adjoint_higher_order_amplitude}). The highest order derivative of $a_{0}$ with respect to $t$ arises from $\partial_{t}(i\partial_{t} a_{N})$ (thus from $\partial_{t}(\left(i\partial_{t}\right)^{N+1} a_{0})$) while the highest order derivative of $x$ arises from $\Delta a_{N}$ (thus from $\Delta^{N+1} a_{0}$). \\ \indent
In $\Vert e^{i\Phi}(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p)a_{N} \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)}$, the highest order derivative of $p$ arises from $\partial^{\alpha}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x}\Delta a_{N}\; (\alpha + |\beta| = m)$ (thus from $\partial^{\alpha}_{t}\partial^{\beta}_{x}\Delta(\Delta^{N - 1}p)\; (\alpha + |\beta| = m)$). The highest order derivative of $a_{0}$ with respect to $t$ arises from $\partial^{m}_{t}(i\partial_{t} a_{N})$ (thus from $\partial^{m}_{t}(\left(i\partial_{t}\right)^{N+1} a_{0})$) while the highest order derivative with respect to $x$ arises from $\partial^{\beta}_{x}\Delta a_{N}\; (|\beta| = m)$ (thus from $\partial^{\beta}_{x}\Delta^{N+1} a_{0}\; (|\beta| = m)$). \\ \indent
Now, by majorizing each factor of $\tau$ or $\xi$ that appears in these derivatives by $\left< \tau, \xi \right>$ and invoking \eqref{leading_order_amplitude_estimate}, we can develop the estimates in \eqref{remainder_estimate_1_step_0} into
\begin{equation} \label{remainder_estimate_0_step_1}
\begin{split}
\Vert r \Vert_{L^{\infty}(Q)} \leq C \rho^{-N+2} \left<\tau,\xi \right>^{2N + 2}h^{-N-2},\\
\Vert r \Vert_{H^{m}(Q)} \leq \tilde{C}\rho^{-N + 2m}\left<\tau, \xi \right>^{2N + m + 2}h^{-(N + m + 1)}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
for constants $C = C\left(\Omega, T, \Vert p \Vert_{W^{2N + 1,\infty}(Q)} \right) > 0$ and $\tilde{C} = \tilde{C}\left(\Omega, T, \Vert p \Vert_{W^{2N + m,\infty}(Q)} \right)$.

\label{vectors_for_other_coefficients}
Henceforth, we will indicate the dependence of the constant $C$ on vector combinations resulting from the product of exponential phases (for example the expression $|(\omega', 0)|$ in \eqref{another_leading_order_amplitude_2}) by writing $\tilde{C}_{j}$, with the understanding that we have chosen a specific set of vectors for the purpose of recovering the coefficient $\tilde{q}_{j}$. If we were concerned with recovering the coefficient $q_{j}$ for a fixed $j \in \{1,2\}$, one could stipulate
\begin{equation}
\omega^{(1)}_{j} + \omega^{(2)}_{j} - \omega^{(3)}_{j} - \omega_{0,j} = 0 \quad \text{and} \quad
-\left|\omega^{(1)}_{j} \right|^{2} - \left|\omega^{(2)}_{j} \right|^{2} + \left|\omega^{(3)}_{j} \right|^{2} + \left|\omega_{0,j} \right|^{2} = 0,
\end{equation}
which ensures that the exponential phase in the $v^{(1)}_{j}v^{(2)}_{j}\overline{v^{(3)}_{j}}\overline{v_{0,j}}$ term vanishes. In order to ensure that the exponential phase for the other terms doesn't vanish, we can additionally stipulate a condition similar to \eqref{CGO_condition_1_1_0} and then pick
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\omega^{(1)}_{j} \coloneqq \eta_{1},\, \omega^{(2)}_{j} \coloneqq -\eta_{1},\\
\omega^{(2)}_{\iota(j)} = \omega^{(3)}_{j} = \omega^{(1)}_{\iota(j)} = \omega^{(3)}_{\iota(j)} \coloneqq \eta_{2},\, \omega_{0,j} = -\eta_{2}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
for some linearly independent vectors $\eta_{1},\eta_{2} \in \mathbb{R}^{n}$.

\label{unique_continuation_result_BC2009}
Suppose $\mathcal{O} \subset \Omega$ is a relatively open neighborhood of $\partial\Omega$ such that $\partial\mathcal{O} \setminus \partial\Omega$ is $C^{2}$. Let $p(x,t) \in L^{\infty}(Q)$ with $p(x,t) = p(x)$ on $\mathcal{O} \times (0, T)$ and $\Gamma$ be a fixed relatively open subset of $\partial \Omega$. Suppose $u(x,t) \in H^{1}(0, T; H^{1}(\Omega))$ is a solution to
\begin{equation} \label{kth_order_linearization_LLZ2023}
\begin{cases}
(i\partial_{t} + \Delta + p(x,t))u = g(x,t) \text{ on } Q,\\
u(x,t) = 0 \text{ on } \Sigma,\\
u(x,0) = 0 \text{ on } \Omega \times \{0\},
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $g(x,t) \in L^{2}(Q)$ is supported in $(\Omega \setminus \mathcal{O}) \times (0, T)$. Then, for any $T^{*} \in (0, T)$, there exist constants $C = C(\Omega, \Gamma, \mathcal{O}, T^{*}, T) > 0, \gamma_{*} > 0$, and $m > 0$ such that
\begin{equation} \label{unique_continuation_statement_BC2009}
\left \Vert u \right \Vert_{L^{2}\left(0, T^{*};H^{1}\left(\Omega_{3}\setminus\Omega_{2}\right)\right)} \leq C \left(\gamma^{-1} + e^{m\gamma} \left \Vert \partial_{\nu}u \right \Vert_{L^{2}(\Gamma \times (0, T))} \right)
\end{equation}
for any $\gamma \geq \gamma_{*}$.

\label{BC2009_unique_continuation_modifications}
\hfill
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Proposition \ref{unique_continuation_result_BC2009} can be proved by following the proof of Lemma $4.6$ in \cite{BC2009}. The proof is based on considering the linear Schr\"{o}dinger equation satisfied by $\chi u$ on $\mathcal{O} \times (0, T)$, where $\chi$ is defined by \eqref{smooth_cut-off_function_0}. The proof also relies on a Carleman estimate with weight function defined on the interval $(-T, T) (\ni t)$. So, we need to extend $\chi u$ to the interval $(-T, T)$, which can be done by defining
\begin{equation*}
u(x,t) = \overline{u(x,-t)} \quad \text{and} \quad g(x,t) = \overline{g(x,-t)}
\end{equation*}
for $(x,t) \in \mathcal{O} \times (-T, 0)$.  Since $p(x,t) = p(x)$ and $g(x,t) = 0$ on $\mathcal{O} \times (0, T)$, the equation satisfied by $\chi u$ then reduces to equation $(4.13)$ in \cite{BC2009}. The rest of the proof then follows as in equations $(4.12)-(4.51)$ in \cite{BC2009}. In fact, the proof in \cite{BC2009} holds under the weaker condition that $\mathcal{O}$ is a relatively open neighborhood of $\Gamma$ rather than the entire boundary. However, the fact that $\mathcal{O}$ is a neighborhood of the entire boundary is essential for other steps towards the proof of the main result in \cite{BC2009} and our proof of Theorem \ref{main_inverse_stability_theorem}. In particular, we need to know that $q_{j,k}$ and $\tilde{q}_{j,k}$ are determined near the entire boundary in order to derive the integral identity \eqref{integral_identity_0}. The reason for the regularity requirement of $\partial\mathcal{O} \setminus \partial\Omega$ is explained in Remark $4.5$ in \cite{AZ2025}.
\item[(b)] Strictly speaking, the inequality in Lemma $4.6$ of \cite{BC2009} estimates $\left \Vert u \right \Vert_{L^{2}\left(0, T_{1};H^{1}\left(\Omega_{3}\setminus\Omega_{2}\right)\right)}$ for a specific choice of $T_{1} \in (0, T)$ such that the constant in \eqref{unique_continuation_statement_BC2009} is independent of $T_{1}$. However, the proof in \cite{BC2009} still holds for any choice of $T^{*} \in (0, T)$ and in this case, the constant that results in \eqref{unique_continuation_statement_BC2009} now depends on $T^{*}$. Also, the estimate in \cite{BC2009} is in terms of $\left \Vert \partial_{\nu}u \right \Vert_{L^{2}(\Gamma \times (-T, T))}$ on the right-hand side. However, this can be majorized by a constant times $\left \Vert \partial_{\nu}u \right \Vert_{L^{2}(\Gamma \times (0, T))}$ in view of how we defined the extended solution in (a).
\item[(c)] Regarding \eqref{unique_continuation_statement_BC2009}, the dependence of the constant on $\Gamma$ is not explicitly mentioned in Lemma $2.1$ from \cite{BC2009}. However, such a dependence is apparent from the Carleman estimate (Lemma $4.2$ in \cite{BC2009}) used to establish the Lemma $2.1$. We refer the reader to Remark $4.6$ in \cite{AZ2025} for further details.
\end{itemize}

\label{real_valued_data_assumptions}
We seek to apply the estimate in \eqref{unique_continuation_statement_BC2009} to the commutator term in \eqref{initial_Fourier_estimate_0_0}. In view of the discussion in Remark \ref{BC2009_unique_continuation_modifications}(a), this requires extending $w^{(1,2,3)}_{j}$ to $\mathcal{O}^{(j)} \times (-T, T)$. We can use the extension defined in Remark \ref{BC2009_unique_continuation_modifications}(a) if $p_{j}, q_{j,k}, \tilde{q}_{j,k}$ are real-valued and if $v^{(l)}_{j}$ (which defines the source terms in \eqref{kth_order_time-dependent_source_terms}) satisfies
